+# Phase 1 Contract: `logredact` Command-Line Interface (v2)
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](../spec.md) | **Plan**: [plan.md](../plan.md) | **Data model**:
+[data-model.md](../data-model.md) | **Supersedes**:
+[001 contract](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/cli.md)
+
+The command line is still this project's only external interface — no library API promised to
+third parties, no network surface. This document supersedes the v1 contract: it states the whole
+interface, with **changed and new clauses marked**, so a test can be written against this file
+alone. Everything unmarked is v1 behaviour reproduced verbatim and is covered by FR-027.
+
+Entity rules are referenced from [data-model.md](../data-model.md) rather than restated.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+**Changed**: `--out-dir DIR` is new (FR-011). Options may appear in any position; the form above
+is conventional, not positional.
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No configuration file, environment variable, or network access
+affects behaviour (SC-008).
+
+> **Note — the `usage:` message does not match this line.** The usage text the tool prints is
+> still `usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]`, deliberately unchanged
+> (research [D-105](../research.md#d-105-the-usage-string-stays-byte-identical); FR-027 and SC-005
+> require the existing usage-line test to pass unchanged). This document, not that string, is the
+> contract for the invocation form.
+
+## Argument grammar
+
+| Token | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `--count` | Enable the tally line. Accepted **in any position** — before, between, or after FILE arguments. Repeating it is equivalent to giving it once. |
+| **`--out-dir`** | **New (FR-011).** Consumes the **immediately following token** as the output folder path. Accepted in any position relative to FILE arguments and `--count`. Given more than once, the **last value wins** (FR-021). With no following token: usage error, exit 2 (FR-012). |
+| **the token after `--out-dir`** | **New.** Taken **literally**, whatever its shape — never inspected for an option-like form. In `--out-dir --count`, the folder is named `--count` and the tally is **not** enabled (FR-011). |
+| `--out-dir=DIR` | **Not recognised.** Falls through to the unrecognised-option error, exit 2 (`001/FR-024`, spec Assumptions). |
+| `-` | An ordinary file path named `-`. Not a stdin convention. |
+| any other token starting with `-` and longer than one character | Unrecognised option → usage error, exit 2. Includes `-h` and `--help`, deliberately not implemented. |
+| anything else | A FILE path, consumed in the order given. |
+
+Parsing is hand-written rather than `argparse`-based (`001/D-007`, research
+[D-104](../research.md#d-104-argument-parsing--index-based-loop-value-token-taken-literally)).
+
+## Modes
+
+The presence of `--out-dir` selects one of two output modes. This is the feature's main structural
+change.
+
+| | **stdout mode** (no `--out-dir`) | **folder mode** (`--out-dir DIR`) |
+|---|---|---|
+| FILE arguments | zero or more | **one or more required** (FR-020) |
+| stdin | read only when no FILE is given | **never read** — zero FILEs is an error (FR-020) |
+| redacted lines go to | stdout | **one file per FILE, inside DIR** (FR-013) |
+| stdout carries | redacted lines, nothing else | **only `wrote N files`** (FR-013, FR-018) |
+| files created | **none, anywhere** (FR-022) | DIR, its missing parents, and one file per FILE |
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Content |
+|---|---|
+| **stdin** | Read **only** in stdout mode with no FILE argument. With at least one FILE, or in folder mode, stdin is never opened. |
+| **stdout** | stdout mode: every input line, redacted, concatenated in argument order, line order preserved within each source — nothing else. **Folder mode: exactly the one line `wrote N files` on success, and nothing at all on any failure** (FR-013, FR-018, FR-026). |
+| **stderr** | The `--count` tally line, and diagnostics. Empty on a successful run without `--count` (FR-010). |
+
+All three text streams are UTF-8 with newline translation disabled, so terminators round-trip
+exactly (`001/D-008`). **Output files use the same settings — that identity is FR-015**
+([R5.4](../data-model.md#5-output-destination--new)).
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|---|---|
+| `0` | All requested input was read and all requested output written. |
+| `2` | Unrecognised option; a FILE could not be read; **`--out-dir` with no value (FR-012)**; **`--out-dir` with no FILE (FR-020)**; **DIR could not be created or used (FR-024)**; **an output file could not be created or written (FR-025)**. All are distinguished by the message, not the status. |
+| `141` (`128+SIGPIPE`) | stdout was closed early, e.g. `| head`. Terminates the way `cat` does, with no traceback and nothing on stderr. In folder mode only the `wrote N files` line can trigger this, so redacted data can never be truncated by it. |
+
+**Changed**: on **every** exit-2 path, neither the `wrote N files` line nor the tally line is
+written, so each of those lines always describes a complete run (FR-026).
+
+## Behavioural contract
+
+### C-1 — Redaction (FR-001…FR-007)
+
+**Changed**: four rules, not three. Emails → `[EMAIL]`, IPv4 addresses → `[IP]`, **international
+phone numbers → `[PHONE]`**, Luhn-valid 13-19 digit card numbers → `[CARD]`. Everything else
+passes through byte for byte.
+
+A **phone number** is a `+` immediately followed by **8 to 15 decimal digits**, with nothing
+between the `+` and the digits and nothing between the digits. The `+` is part of the replacement.
+
+| Input | Output | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+14155552671`, `+442071838750` | `[PHONE]` | 11 and 12 digits (FR-002) |
+| `+12345678` | `[PHONE]` | exactly 8 — lower bound |
+| `+123456789012345` | `[PHONE]` | exactly 15 — upper bound |
+| `+1234567` | unchanged | 7 digits, too few (FR-005) |
+| `+1234567890123456` | unchanged | 16 digits — **never partially replaced** (FR-004) |
+| `100+25` | unchanged | no 8-digit run |
+| `100+14155552671` | `100[PHONE]` | what precedes the `+` is unconstrained (FR-005) |
+| `++14155552671` | `+[PHONE]` | the match starts at the second `+` |
+| `+1 415 555 2671`, `+1-415-555-2671` | unchanged | separators are not permitted |
+| `+1.2.3.4` | `+[IP]` | not a phone; the IPv4 rule applies |
+| `[PHONE]` in the input | unchanged, uncounted | placeholders are never re-examined |
+
+**Precedence** (FR-006): each character is redacted at most once; the leftmost match wins; at an
+equal start position the order is **email → IPv4 → phone → card**.
+
+| Input | Output | Clause |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+12345678@b.io` | `[EMAIL]` | email beats phone at an equal start — the domain is not left exposed |
+| `jane@example.com+14155552671` | `[EMAIL][PHONE]` | adjacent, non-overlapping |
+| `+4111111111111111` | `+[CARD]` | 16 digits, so not a phone; Luhn-valid, so a card |
+| `a@b.io 10.0.0.7 4111 1111 1111 1111 +442071838750` | `[EMAIL] [IP] [CARD] [PHONE]` | all four on one line |
+
+Recognition criteria and the full precedence argument are in
+[data-model.md §3](../data-model.md#3-redaction-rule--a-fourth-member).
+
+### C-2 — Tally output (FR-008…FR-010)
+
+**Changed.** With `--count`, exactly one line is written to stderr **after all stdout output**:
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N
+```
+
+Four figures in that order. This **replaces** the three-figure `emails=N ips=N cards=N` form;
+there is no option that restores it. One tally per run across all sources combined; occurrences,
+not distinct values; `0` where none occurred. Suppressed on every exit-2 path (FR-026).
+
+In folder mode the tally still comes after all stdout, i.e. after the `wrote N files` line
+(FR-019).
+
+### C-3 — Output folder (FR-013…FR-018)
+
+With `--out-dir DIR` and at least one FILE:
+
+1. **DIR is created first**, with any missing parent directories, before the first FILE is opened.
+   An existing DIR is used as it is and unrelated files in it are untouched (FR-016).
+2. Each FILE's redacted lines are written to `DIR/{basename(FILE)}` — **the final path component
+   only**, so `logs/2026/app.log` → `DIR/app.log` and input directories are not reproduced inside
+   DIR (FR-014).
+3. Content is **byte-identical** to what that FILE's redacted lines would have been on stdout:
+   line order, CRLF terminators, a missing final newline, and encoding all preserved (FR-015).
+4. An existing file of that name in DIR is **truncated and replaced entirely**, with no leftover
+   trailing bytes (FR-017).
+5. **No redacted line reaches stdout** (FR-013), and there are no per-file progress messages.
+6. On success, exactly one line to stdout, then exit 0 (FR-018):
+
+   ```text
+   wrote N files
+   ```
+
+   N is **one per FILE argument processed**. The noun is fixed — a single FILE gives
+   `wrote 1 files`.
+
+Resolved consequences, each specified rather than accidental:
+
+| Situation | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `a/app.log` and `b/app.log` together | both map to `DIR/app.log`, processed in argument order; the second's content survives; `wrote 2 files` |
+| the same path given twice | read twice, written twice, `wrote 2 files` |
+| a readable but empty FILE | an empty output file is created and counted |
+| DIR is an input's own directory | the output file **replaces the input while it is being read**; the result is not well defined and the input is destroyed. An unguarded hazard (spec Assumptions) |
+| writes interrupted part-way | no atomicity: a partially written output file may remain; the non-zero exit status is the signal |
+
+### C-4 — Diagnostics (FR-012, FR-020, FR-023…FR-025)
+
+Every diagnostic keeps the one-line shape `logredact: {subject}: {reason}`. Reason text for OS
+failures comes from the OS (`strerror`) or the decoder, so tests assert the subject plus a stable
+substring, not the full string (`001/D-009`).
+
+**Unchanged** — read failure, naming the FILE (FR-023):
+
+```text
+logredact: missing.log: No such file or directory
+```
+
+Also `logredact: /tmp: Is a directory`, `logredact: secret.log: Permission denied`, and for
+undecodable bytes the decoder's own message. In folder mode this is reached **before** an output
+file is created for that FILE, so no stray empty file is left behind
+([R7.4](../data-model.md#7-diagnostics-and-exit-status)).
+
+**Unchanged** — unrecognised option, nothing read, nothing on stdout:
+
+```text
+usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+logredact: unrecognized option: {token}
+```
+
+**New** — `--out-dir` as the final token with no value (FR-012): nothing read, nothing on stdout:
+
+```text
+usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+logredact: --out-dir requires a directory
+```
+
+**New** — `--out-dir` with no FILE argument (FR-020): stdin not read, stdout empty, no directory
+created:
+
+```text
+logredact: --out-dir requires at least one FILE
+```
+
+**New** — DIR cannot be created or used as a directory (FR-024), naming the path **as the user
+gave it**:
+
+```text
+logredact: clean: File exists
+logredact: out/2026/clean: File exists
+logredact: /proc/nope: Permission denied
+```
+
+**New** — an output file cannot be created or written (FR-025), naming the **output** path:
+
+```text
+logredact: clean/app.log: Is a directory
+```
+
+The usage line accompanies **malformed command lines only** (unrecognised option, missing
+`--out-dir` value). The three semantic failures above are reported by their diagnostic alone
+([R7.1](../data-model.md#7-diagnostics-and-exit-status)).
+
+### C-5 — Failure sequencing (FR-023, FR-026)
+
+Processing stops at the first failure of any kind. Later FILE arguments are not opened. Output
+files already written are left in place, as already-written stdout is — it is already-sanitised
+data and is not retracted. **Neither `wrote N files` nor the tally is printed**, with `--count` or
+without. Exit status 2.
+
+Validation order is strict, so a bad command line never touches the filesystem:
+**parse → check `--out-dir` has a FILE → create DIR → read inputs**
+(research [D-106](../research.md#d-106-order-of-operations--validate-fully-then-create-the-folder-then-read)).
+
+## Worked examples (normative — these are the SC-001 cases)
+
+`$` lines are the command; `2>` marks stderr. The three examples the feature description gives are
+**W-1**, **W-2**, and **W-3**.
+
+**W-1 — phone redaction on a piped stream**
+
+```console
+$ echo 'call +14155552671 now' | python -m logredact
+call [PHONE] now
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+**W-2 — the four-figure tally**
+
+```console
+$ echo 'a@b.io +442071838750' | python -m logredact --count
+[EMAIL] [PHONE]
+2> emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+**W-3 — output folder**
+
+```console
+$ cat app.log
+user jane@example.com
+$ cat web.log
+GET / from 10.0.0.7
+$ python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log web.log
+wrote 2 files
+$ cat clean/app.log
+user [EMAIL]
+$ cat clean/web.log
+GET / from [IP]
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+`clean/` is created if absent, as are any missing parents.
+
+**W-4 — basename only, directories not reproduced**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact --out-dir clean logs/2026/app.log
+wrote 1 files
+$ ls clean
+app.log
+```
+
+Note `wrote 1 files` — the plural noun is fixed (FR-018).
+
+**W-5 — folder mode with a tally, both streams**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact --count --out-dir clean app.log web.log
+wrote 2 files
+2> emails=1 ips=1 cards=0 phones=0
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+One tally covering all files, after the stdout line (FR-019).
+
+**W-6 — unreadable FILE mid-list, in folder mode**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log missing.log --count
+2> logredact: missing.log: No such file or directory
+$ echo $?
+2
+$ ls clean
+app.log
+```
+
+`clean/app.log` was written and stays written; no `wrote N files` line, no tally, and no stray
+`clean/missing.log` (FR-023, FR-026).
+
+**W-7 — `--out-dir` with no value**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact app.log --out-dir
+2> usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+2> logredact: --out-dir requires a directory
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+stdout is empty; `app.log` is never opened (FR-012).
+
+**W-8 — `--out-dir` with no FILE**
+
+```console
+$ echo 'a@b.io' | python -m logredact --out-dir clean
+2> logredact: --out-dir requires at least one FILE
+$ echo $?
+2
+$ ls clean
+ls: cannot access 'clean': No such file or directory
+```
+
+stdin is not read, stdout is empty, and `clean` is not created (FR-020).
+
+**W-9 — folder path that is an existing regular file**
+
+```console
+$ touch clean
+$ python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log
+2> logredact: clean: File exists
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+No `wrote N files` line; `app.log` is never opened (FR-024).
+
+**W-10 — option-shaped folder value**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact --out-dir --count app.log
+wrote 1 files
+$ ls -d ./--count
+./--count
+$ cat ./--count/app.log
+user [EMAIL]
+```
+
+The token after `--out-dir` is the folder name; `--count` is consumed as that value, so no tally
+is printed and stderr is empty (FR-011).
+
+**W-11 — no `--out-dir`: today's behaviour, unchanged**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact app.log web.log
+user [EMAIL]
+GET / from [IP]
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+No `wrote N files` line and no file created anywhere (FR-022).
+
+**W-12 — v1 examples still hold (FR-027, SC-005)**
+
+Every worked example in the [v1 contract](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/cli.md) — E-1 through
+E-7 — reproduces unchanged, **except E-3's tally line**, which becomes
+`emails=1 ips=1 cards=0 phones=0` under C-2.
+
+## Out of scope for this contract
+
+No `--help`, no `--out-dir=DIR` form, no `--out-dir` with stdin, no preserved input directory
+structure inside DIR, no atomic or backup writes, no in-place editing, no output-file naming
+scheme other than the input's own file name, no per-file progress output, no configurable
+placeholders, no self-overwrite guard, and no phone formats beyond `+` followed by 8-15 digits —
+no internal separators, no extensions, no national formats without a `+`. Also still out of scope:
+IPv6, names, URLs, API keys, structured-log awareness, pseudonymisation, and allowlisting (spec
+"Out of scope").
